# ============================================================
# ITBIS — Insider Threat Behavioral Intelligence System
# Cursor Rules & Code-Generation Standards
# ============================================================

## Project Overview
You are assisting with an AI-powered Insider Threat Behavioral Intelligence System (ITBIS).
Backend: FastAPI (Python 3.11+)
Frontend: Next.js 14 (App Router) + Tailwind CSS + TypeScript (strict)

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1. DESIGN SYSTEM — Dark-Mode Cybersecurity Theme
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## Color Palette
- Background (base):      #0B0F19   (deep navy-black)
- Card / container:       #161C2E   (dark navy)
- Elevated surface:       #1E2640   (mid navy)
- Border / divider:       #2A3352   (muted navy)
- Primary accent:         #3B82F6   (electric blue)
- Secondary accent:       #6366F1   (indigo)
- Danger / alert:         #EF4444   (red)
- Warning:                #F59E0B   (amber)
- Success / safe:         #10B981   (emerald)
- Text primary:           #E2E8F0   (light slate)
- Text secondary:         #94A3B8   (muted slate)
- Text muted:             #475569   (dim slate)

## Typography
- Font family: 'Inter', 'JetBrains Mono' (monospace for code/IDs), system-ui, sans-serif
- Font scale: compact — use text-xs / text-sm for dense data tables
- Headings: font-semibold or font-bold, tracking-tight

## Layout Principles
- High-density, compact layout — minimise vertical whitespace
- Sidebar navigation: fixed, 240 px wide, #161C2E background
- Top header bar: 56 px tall, #0B0F19 with border-b border-[#2A3352]
- Data tables: rows 36 px tall, alternating #0B0F19 / #161C2E
- Cards: rounded-lg border border-[#2A3352] bg-[#161C2E] p-4 shadow-lg
- Modals / drawers: backdrop-blur-sm bg-black/50 overlay

## Tailwind CSS Config Extensions (tailwind.config.ts)
colors:
  bg-base:        '#0B0F19'
  bg-card:        '#161C2E'
  bg-elevated:    '#1E2640'
  border-subtle:  '#2A3352'
  accent-blue:    '#3B82F6'
  accent-indigo:  '#6366F1'
  danger:         '#EF4444'
  warning:        '#F59E0B'
  success:        '#10B981'
  text-primary:   '#E2E8F0'
  text-secondary: '#94A3B8'
  text-muted:     '#475569'

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 2. TYPESCRIPT — Strict Mode Rules
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

- Always enable strict: true in tsconfig.json
- No implicit any — all function parameters and return types must be explicitly typed
- Prefer interface over type for object shapes; use type for unions/intersections
- Use const assertions and satisfies operator where appropriate
- All async functions must have explicit Promise<T> return types
- Discriminated unions for API response states: { status: 'loading' | 'success' | 'error' }
- Enums: prefer const enum or plain union string literals over numeric enums
- Never use any; use unknown and narrow with type guards

## Naming Conventions
- Components:    PascalCase  (e.g. ThreatTimeline.tsx)
- Hooks:         camelCase prefixed with use  (e.g. useThreatAlerts.ts)
- API routes:    kebab-case  (e.g. /api/v1/threat-events)
- Types/Interfaces: PascalCase with descriptive suffix (e.g. ThreatEventPayload)
- Constants:     SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 3. NEXT.JS 14 APP ROUTER RULES
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

- Use App Router (app/ directory) exclusively — no pages/ directory
- Server Components by default; add 'use client' only when necessary
- Data fetching: use React Server Components + fetch with cache/revalidate options
- Route groups: (auth), (dashboard), (admin) for layout isolation
- Loading states: always provide loading.tsx skeleton screens
- Error states: always provide error.tsx with retry capability
- Metadata: export metadata object from every page.tsx
- Images: always use next/image with explicit width, height, and alt

## Component Structure
- Co-locate component styles using Tailwind class variants — no external CSS modules
- Compound components for complex UI (e.g. <DataTable>, <AlertBadge>)
- Wrap third-party charts/visualisations in a client boundary component

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 4. FASTAPI BACKEND RULES
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

- Python 3.11+ with full type annotations on every function
- Pydantic v2 models for all request/response schemas
- Use pydantic-settings for environment configuration (BaseSettings)
- SQLAlchemy 2.x (async) for PostgreSQL; Motor for MongoDB async access
- JWT authentication via python-jose; password hashing via passlib[bcrypt]
- All endpoints under /api/v1/ prefix
- Router separation: one APIRouter per domain (users, alerts, events, analytics)
- Dependency injection for DB sessions and current user
- Never return raw SQLAlchemy model objects — always convert to Pydantic schema
- HTTP status codes: 200 OK, 201 Created, 204 No Content, 400 Bad Request,
  401 Unauthorized, 403 Forbidden, 404 Not Found, 422 Unprocessable Entity, 500 Internal Server Error
- Global exception handlers for HTTPException and unhandled errors

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 5. SECURITY & THREAT INTELLIGENCE DOMAIN RULES
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

- All sensitive fields (tokens, passwords, PII) must be excluded from logs
- Risk score values: float 0.0–1.0, displayed as percentage in UI
- Threat severity levels: CRITICAL | HIGH | MEDIUM | LOW | INFO
- Alert statuses: OPEN | ACKNOWLEDGED | INVESTIGATING | RESOLVED | FALSE_POSITIVE
- Behavioral anomaly scores must include confidence interval metadata
- User entity IDs should be UUIDs, never sequential integers in API responses

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 6. CODE QUALITY
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

- ESLint + Prettier for frontend (airbnb-typescript base)
- Ruff + Black for Python backend
- All new features must include unit tests (pytest for backend, Vitest for frontend)
- No console.log in production code — use a structured logger
- Commit messages: Conventional Commits format (feat:, fix:, chore:, docs:, test:)
